/* ===== Module 1 ===== */
COURSE.push({
id: 'm1', short: 'The big picture', title: 'Test automation and Playwright: the big picture', minutes: 35,
lead: 'What automation is for, what it cannot do, where UI tests fit among other tests, and how Playwright is built, with one picture you will keep using for the whole course.',
learn: ['say what a good candidate for UI automation looks like, and what is better left manual', 'place a check on the right layer: unit, API or UI', 'name the four Playwright objects (driver, browser, context, page) and say what a context is for', 'explain why the Python and Kotlin tracks look different but work the same way'],
body: `
<h2>Why automate at all</h2>
<p>A manual tester can check one flow in a few minutes. Before every release there may be forty such flows. Doing them by hand each time is slow, boring and easy to get wrong on the thirtieth repetition. An automated test is a <b>very literal robot colleague</b>: it follows the same steps in the same order every time, never gets tired, and tells you in minutes whether something that used to work has broken.</p>
<p>What a robot is not good at is <b>noticing the unexpected</b>. It checks only what you told it to check. This is why automation does not replace testers. It frees them from repetition, so that they can spend time on exploring, on thinking up risks, and on the things a script cannot judge, like "this page feels confusing".</p>
${tbl(['Good candidate for automation', 'Better left manual'], [
  ['A check you repeat on every build (login, search, checkout)', 'A brand new feature that is still changing every day'],
  ['A stable, critical flow where a failure is expensive', 'Exploratory testing: looking for problems nobody predicted'],
  ['Many data variations of the same steps', 'Judging look and feel, wording or usability'],
  ['A check that is tedious and error-prone for a person', 'A check you will run only once']
])}
<h2>The test pyramid: choose the cheapest layer that can catch the problem</h2>
<p>Tests come in layers. <b>Unit tests</b> check one small piece of code and run in milliseconds. <b>API tests</b> talk to the server directly and check its answers. <b>UI tests</b> drive a real browser through real pages. The higher the layer, the more of the whole system it proves, and the slower, more fragile and more expensive it is.</p>
<p>A healthy project has many fast tests at the bottom and a few UI tests on top, for the journeys that matter most. Playwright is mostly a UI tool, but it can also call APIs, which makes it useful for the middle layer too (module 9).</p>
{{w:pyramid}}
<h2>How Playwright is built</h2>
<p>Playwright is a library that controls real browsers (Chromium, Firefox and WebKit) from your test code. Four objects are enough to understand the whole picture. Think of an apartment building:</p>
${tbl(['Object', 'In the building', 'What it is', 'What it is for'], [
  ['Playwright', 'The caretaker', 'The driver that starts browsers and speaks to them', 'You create it once and close it at the end'],
  ['Browser', 'The building', 'One running browser program', 'Expensive to start, so it is shared by many tests'],
  ['Browser context', 'One private flat', 'An isolated profile with its own cookies, storage and logins', 'Cheap to create. <b>Every test gets its own</b>, so tests cannot affect each other'],
  ['Page', 'A window in the flat', 'One tab', 'This is where you click and type. Most of the code you write talks to a page']
])}
${co('The idea to keep', 'A fresh context per test is the reason Playwright tests can run in any order and in parallel. Nothing one test does (a login, a cookie, a saved todo) is visible to the next test, unless you decide it should be.')}
<h2>Two tracks, one idea</h2>
<p>This course has two tracks. The <b>Python</b> track uses the Playwright library for Python with <code>pytest</code>, which creates the browser, the context and the page for you through a ready-made plugin. The <b>Kotlin</b> track uses the Playwright library for Java (Playwright has no separate Kotlin library, and the Java one works from Kotlin without any wrapper) with <code>JUnit</code> and <code>Gradle</code>. There, a small base class creates the browser, the context and the page, and plays the role that the pytest plugin plays in Python.</p>
${tbl(['', 'Python track', 'Kotlin track'], [
  ['Test runner', 'pytest', 'JUnit (Jupiter)'],
  ['Names of methods', 'snake_case: <code>get_by_role</code>', 'camelCase: <code>getByRole</code>'],
  ['Open a page', '<code>page.goto(url)</code>', '<code>page.navigate(url)</code>'],
  ['Assertion', '<code>expect(locator).to_be_visible()</code>', '<code>assertThat(locator).isVisible()</code>'],
  ['Options', 'Named arguments: <code>name="Save"</code>', 'Option objects: <code>Page.GetByRoleOptions().setName("Save")</code>'],
  ['Browser, context, page', 'Fixtures from the plugin', 'A base class you write once']
])}
<p>Everything else, the locators, the auto-waiting, the isolation and the debugging tools, is the same. If you learn one track well, the other takes an afternoon.</p>
<h2>Watch your first test being written</h2>
<p>Press Play. The page on the right is a practice page that works like the public TodoMVC demo, which you will use on your own computer in the next module.</p>
{{watch:s1}}
`,
takeaways: ['Automate repetitive, stable, valuable checks. Keep exploring and judging for humans.', 'Put each check on the cheapest layer that can catch the problem: unit, then API, then a few UI tests.', 'Playwright objects: driver, browser, context (an isolated profile), page (a tab).', 'One fresh context per test keeps tests independent.', 'Python and Kotlin differ in names and in how the browser is created, not in the ideas.'],
quiz: [
  { q: 'What is a browser context?', o: ['An isolated browser profile with its own cookies and storage, cheap to create', 'A second copy of the browser program', 'A single browser tab', 'A file with test data'], a: 0, why: 'A context is like a private flat inside the building (the browser). It is cheap, and it keeps tests apart from each other.' },
  { q: 'A check must confirm that an email validator rejects "a@". Which layer is the best place for it?', o: ['A unit test', 'An API test', 'A UI test', 'A manual test only'], a: 0, why: 'It is one small rule with many inputs. A unit test is the fastest and the most precise way to cover it.' },
  { type: 'multi', q: 'Which of these are good candidates for UI automation?', o: ['A login and checkout smoke check run on every build', 'A stable regression flow where a failure is expensive', 'A new feature that changes every day', 'Exploratory testing of an unfamiliar area', 'A long repetitive flow that people find tedious'], a: [0, 1, 4], why: 'Automation pays off for repeated, stable, valuable or tedious flows. A moving feature breaks the script daily, and exploration needs a human mind.' },
  { type: 'fill', q: 'In Playwright, what is the object that represents a single browser tab called? (one word)', ans: ['page', 'Page'], why: 'The page is where you click, type and read. It lives inside a browser context.' },
  { type: 'order', q: 'Put the Playwright objects in order, from the largest to the smallest.', items: ['Playwright (the driver)', 'Browser', 'Browser context', 'Page'], why: 'The driver starts a browser, the browser holds contexts, and each context holds pages.' },
  { q: 'Why does every test get its own browser context?', o: ['So that tests cannot affect each other and can run in any order', 'Because a page cannot be reused', 'Because it is required by the browser vendor', 'To make tests slower on purpose'], a: 0, why: 'Cookies, logins and saved data live in the context. A new one for every test means no leftovers.' },
  { q: 'What does the Kotlin track use to control the browser?', o: ['The official Playwright for Java library, called from Kotlin', 'A separate Playwright for Kotlin library', 'The Python library through a bridge', 'The JavaScript library through Node'], a: 0, why: 'Playwright has no Kotlin-specific library. Kotlin works with the Java one directly.' }
],
practice: { id: 'p-m1', title: 'Choose what to automate',
  brief: '<p>Below are eight manual test cases for an online bank demo. Choose the <b>three</b> that you would automate first, and explain each choice in one or two sentences. Use these criteria: how often it runs, how much it costs when it fails, how stable the feature is, and whether a person finds it tedious.</p><ol><li>Log in with a valid user and see the accounts overview.</li><li>Log in with a wrong password and see an error.</li><li>Check that the new logo looks good on a phone.</li><li>Transfer money between two accounts and see both balances change.</li><li>Register a new user with all fields filled in.</li><li>Look for unusual behaviour when a transfer is made with a very long amount.</li><li>Check the wording of the legal notice in the footer.</li><li>Open a new savings account and see it in the list.</li></ol>',
  template: 'My three choices:\n1. \n2. \n3. \n\nWhy these three:\n\nWhat I leave manual, and why:\n',
  checks: ['I chose cases that run often and fail expensively', 'For each choice I gave a reason, not just a title', 'I left at least one case manual on purpose', 'I named the layer (UI, API or unit) that would suit a case best'],
  model: '<p><b>A reasonable choice:</b> (1) log in and see the accounts overview, because every other flow depends on it and it runs on every build; (4) transfer money, because it is the core business function and a failure is expensive; (8) open a new account, a stable flow with several steps that is tedious by hand.</p><p><b>Left manual:</b> (3) a judgement of how something looks on a phone, (6) exploring unusual input, which needs a curious human, and (7) wording, which a script cannot judge.</p><p><b>Layers:</b> the wrong-password case (2) is also worth automating, but the rule itself is better checked at the API layer, and a single UI test is enough to prove the message is shown.</p>' }
});

