COURSE.push({
id: 'm2', short: 'Setup and first test', title: 'Set up your machine and run your first test', minutes: 60,
lead: 'Install the tools, run a real test against a public demo site, watch the browser do the work, break the test on purpose, and learn to read the error.',
learn: ['set up a working Playwright project on your own computer in your chosen language', 'run a test, watch it in a visible browser, and slow it down', 'read a failure message and find the first useful line', 'use codegen to explore an unfamiliar page'],
body: `
<h2>What you will build</h2>
<p>A tiny project with one test. It opens the public <a href="https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TodoMVC demo</a> (a to-do list made for practising testing), adds a todo and checks that it appears. Small, but it is a complete, real test, and the same skeleton is used for the rest of the course.</p>
${co('Versions used in this course', 'The code was run with these versions in November 2026. Newer ones will probably work, but if something behaves differently, install exactly these first.')}
${tbl(['Part', L('Python track', 'Kotlin track')], [
  ['Language', L('Python 3.10 or newer (tested with 3.12)', 'JDK 17 or newer (tested with 21), Kotlin 2.4.21')],
  ['Playwright', L('playwright 1.64.0', 'com.microsoft.playwright:playwright 1.64.0')],
  ['Test runner', L('pytest with pytest-playwright 0.10.0', 'JUnit Jupiter 6.1.3 (the same annotations as JUnit 5)')],
  ['Build tool', L('pip and a virtual environment', 'Gradle 9.9 (the wrapper downloads it)')]
])}
<h2>Step by step: set it up on your computer</h2>
<p>Tick each step when you have done it. Every step says <b>what</b> to do and <b>why</b>, so that you can fix things yourself when your machine is different from mine.</p>
${steps('m2', [
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Check that Python is installed: run <code>python --version</code> (on Windows you can also try <code>py --version</code>).', code: sh('python --version'), why: 'Playwright for Python needs Python 3.10 or newer. It is better to find out now than from a confusing error later.', see: 'A line such as <code>Python 3.12.10</code>. If the command is not found, install Python from python.org and tick "Add Python to PATH".' },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Create a folder for the course and a virtual environment inside it.', code: sh('mkdir playwright-course\ncd playwright-course\npython -m venv .venv'), why: 'A virtual environment is a private box of packages for this project. Without it, packages from different projects mix up and break each other.', see: 'A new <code>.venv</code> folder.' },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Activate the environment.', code: sh({ py: '# Windows (PowerShell or cmd)\n.venv\\Scripts\\activate\n\n# macOS and Linux\nsource .venv/bin/activate', label: 'Terminal' }), why: 'Activation tells the terminal to use the packages in <code>.venv</code>. You must do it again each time you open a new terminal.', see: 'The prompt starts with <code>(.venv)</code>.' },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Create the file <code>requirements.txt</code> and install the packages.', code: code({ py: 'playwright==1.64.0\npytest-playwright==0.10.0', file: { py: 'requirements.txt' }, ex: 'deps' }), why: 'Pinning exact versions means your code behaves like mine, today and in a year. The second package is the pytest plugin that gives you the <code>page</code> fixture.', see: 'After <code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code>, the last line says that both packages were installed.' },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Install the browser that Playwright will control.', code: sh('pip install -r requirements.txt\nplaywright install chromium'), why: 'Playwright drives its own copy of the browser, so that tests behave the same on every machine. The library and the browser are installed separately.', see: 'A progress bar that downloads Chromium (about 150 MB).' },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Create <code>pytest.ini</code> and the test file <code>tests/test_first.py</code>.', code: code({ py: 'from playwright.sync_api import Page, expect\n\n\ndef test_adds_a_todo(page: Page):\n    page.goto("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n\n    new_todo = page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?")\n    new_todo.fill("Buy milk")\n    new_todo.press("Enter")\n\n    expect(page.get_by_test_id("todo-title")).to_have_text("Buy milk")', file: { py: 'tests/test_first.py' }, ex: 'first' }), why: 'pytest finds tests in files named <code>test_*.py</code>, in functions named <code>test_*</code>. The argument <code>page</code> is a fixture: pytest-playwright creates a fresh browser context and page and hands it to your test. In <code>pytest.ini</code>, put <code>[pytest]</code> and <code>testpaths = tests</code>.', see: 'Nothing yet. This step only creates files.' },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Run the test.', code: sh('pytest'), why: 'This is the moment of truth: a robot opens a browser in the background, does the steps, and reports.', see: '<code>1 passed</code> in a second or two.' },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Run it again, and watch the browser work.', code: sh('pytest --headed --slowmo 700'), why: 'By default the browser is invisible (headless), which is fast. Seeing the robot is the best way to understand what it does. <code>--slowmo 700</code> adds 700 ms between actions, so that you can follow.', see: 'A Chromium window opens, the text appears in the input, and the todo is added.' },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Break the test on purpose: change the expected text to <code>"Buy bread"</code> and run it again. Then change it back.', why: 'You will meet failures every day. Reading one calmly, while you know the cause, is the best practice.', see: 'The test fails after about 5 seconds. Find the lines <b>Expected</b> and <b>Actual</b>, and the Call log below them.' },
  { lang: 'py', do: 'Try the recorder: run <code>playwright codegen https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/</code>, click around, and watch the code appear.', code: sh('playwright codegen https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/'), why: 'Codegen writes the code for what you do and suggests locators. Treat it as a helpful hint, not as finished code: you will clean it up.', see: 'Two windows: a browser, and the Playwright Inspector with generated code.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Check that Java is installed: run <code>java -version</code>.', code: sh('java -version'), why: 'The Kotlin track runs on the JVM, and the Playwright library for Java needs a JDK, version 17 or newer. If the command is missing, install Temurin 21 (Adoptium).', see: 'A line such as <code>openjdk version "21.0.x"</code>.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Create a project in IntelliJ IDEA (the free Community edition is enough): New Project, language Kotlin, build system Gradle, Gradle DSL Kotlin, JDK 21. Name it <code>playwright-course</code>.', why: 'IntelliJ understands Kotlin and Gradle, so you get auto-complete for the whole Playwright API, which makes learning much faster. It also creates the Gradle wrapper (<code>gradlew</code>) for you.', see: 'An empty project with <code>build.gradle.kts</code> and <code>src</code>.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Replace the content of <code>build.gradle.kts</code>, then reload the Gradle project (the small elephant icon, or "Load Gradle Changes").', code: code({ kt: 'plugins {\n    kotlin("jvm") version "2.4.21"\n}\n\nrepositories {\n    mavenCentral()\n}\n\ndependencies {\n    testImplementation("com.microsoft.playwright:playwright:1.64.0")\n    testImplementation(platform("org.junit:junit-bom:6.1.3"))\n    testImplementation("org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter")\n    testRuntimeOnly("org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher")\n}\n\ntasks.test {\n    useJUnitPlatform()\n    testLogging {\n        events("passed", "failed", "skipped")\n        showStandardStreams = true\n    }\n}\n\n// Runs the Playwright command-line tool, for example:\n//   ./gradlew playwright --args="codegen https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/"\ntasks.register<JavaExec>("playwright") {\n    classpath = sourceSets["test"].runtimeClasspath\n    mainClass.set("com.microsoft.playwright.CLI")\n}', file: { kt: 'build.gradle.kts' }, ex: 'deps' }), why: 'This file lists the libraries the project uses (Playwright and JUnit), pinned to exact versions. <code>testImplementation</code> means "only for tests". The last block adds a shortcut to the Playwright command-line tool, which you will use for codegen.', see: 'Gradle downloads the libraries. The red "unresolved" marks in the editor disappear.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Create the base class <code>BaseTest.kt</code> in <code>src/test/kotlin/course</code>.', code: code({ kt: 'package course\n\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.Browser\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.BrowserContext\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.Page\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.Playwright\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.AfterAll\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.AfterEach\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeAll\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.TestInstance\n\n@TestInstance(TestInstance.Lifecycle.PER_CLASS)\nopen class BaseTest {\n    lateinit var playwright: Playwright\n    lateinit var browser: Browser\n    lateinit var context: BrowserContext\n    lateinit var page: Page\n\n    @BeforeAll\n    fun launchBrowser() {\n        playwright = Playwright.create()\n        browser = playwright.chromium().launch()\n    }\n\n    @AfterAll\n    fun closeBrowser() {\n        browser.close()\n        playwright.close()\n    }\n\n    @BeforeEach\n    fun createContext() {\n        context = browser.newContext()\n        page = context.newPage()\n    }\n\n    @AfterEach\n    fun closeContext() {\n        context.close()\n    }\n}', file: { kt: 'src/test/kotlin/course/BaseTest.kt' }, ex: 'base' }), why: 'In Python the pytest plugin does this for you. In Kotlin you write it once. The browser is started once per test class (expensive), and a fresh context and page are created for each test (cheap), exactly the picture from module 1. Module 6 explains the annotations.', see: 'Nothing runs yet.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Create the test class <code>FirstTest.kt</code> in the same folder.', code: code({ kt: 'package course\n\nimport com.microsoft.playwright.assertions.PlaywrightAssertions.assertThat\nimport org.junit.jupiter.api.Test\n\nclass FirstTest : BaseTest() {\n    @Test\n    fun addsATodo() {\n        page.navigate("https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/")\n\n        val newTodo = page.getByPlaceholder("What needs to be done?")\n        newTodo.fill("Buy milk")\n        newTodo.press("Enter")\n\n        assertThat(page.getByTestId("todo-title")).hasText("Buy milk")\n    }\n}', file: { kt: 'src/test/kotlin/course/FirstTest.kt' }, ex: 'first' }), why: '<code>: BaseTest()</code> means "this class inherits the browser, context and page". <code>@Test</code> marks a function that JUnit should run. Gradle looks for tests in <code>src/test</code>.', see: 'A green run icon next to the class and the function.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Run the test: click the green icon, or run <code>./gradlew test</code> in the terminal (on Windows <code>gradlew.bat test</code>).', code: sh('./gradlew test'), why: 'The first run is slow on purpose: the Playwright library downloads its browsers (a few hundred MB) and Gradle downloads itself. Later runs take seconds. If you see a warning about missing libraries for WebKit, ignore it: this course uses Chromium.', see: '<code>FirstTest > addsATodo() PASSED</code> and <code>BUILD SUCCESSFUL</code>.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Watch the browser work: change the launch line in <code>BaseTest.kt</code> and run again.', code: code({ kt: 'browser = playwright.chromium().launch(\n    BrowserType.LaunchOptions().setHeadless(false).setSlowMo(700.0)\n)', label: 'Replace the launch line (add import com.microsoft.playwright.BrowserType)' }), why: 'By default the browser is invisible (headless), which is fast. Seeing the robot is the best way to understand what it does. The slow motion adds 700 ms between actions, so that you can follow.', see: 'A Chromium window opens, the text appears in the input, and the todo is added.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Break the test on purpose: change the expected text to <code>"Buy bread"</code> and run it again. Then change it back.', why: 'You will meet failures every day. Reading one calmly, while you know the cause, is the best practice.', see: 'The test fails after about 5 seconds. Find the lines about the expected and the actual text, and the Call log below them.' },
  { lang: 'kt', do: 'Try the recorder with the shortcut task from <code>build.gradle.kts</code>.', code: sh('./gradlew playwright --args="codegen https://demo.playwright.dev/todomvc/"'), why: 'Codegen writes the code for what you do and suggests locators. Treat it as a helpful hint, not as finished code: you will clean it up.', see: 'Two windows: a browser, and the Playwright Inspector with generated Java code (you will translate it to Kotlin by changing a few lines).' }
])}
{{w:errdecoder}}
<h2>Reading a failure</h2>
<p>A failing Playwright test prints a long message. You do not need to read all of it. Read in this order: (1) the <b>first line</b>, which says what kind of problem it is (a timeout, an assertion, a strict mode violation); (2) <b>Expected</b> and <b>Actual</b> for assertions; (3) the <b>Call log</b>, which is a diary of what Playwright was doing: what it was waiting for, and what it found. Most of the time the call log already tells you the answer.</p>
<h2>Try it in the browser first</h2>
<p>Before (or after) you set up your own machine, write the same first test in the lab. It runs a simulation of the page, so you can experiment without installing anything.</p>
{{lab:l2a}}
`,
takeaways: ['Pin the versions of Playwright and the test runner, so that the code behaves the same everywhere.', 'The library and the browsers are installed separately. Playwright drives its own browser copies.', 'Python gets browser, context and page from the pytest plugin. Kotlin gets them from a base class you write once.', 'Watch the robot with headed mode and slow motion. Break a test on purpose to learn what failures look like.', 'Read failures in the order: first line, Expected and Actual, Call log.'],
quiz: [
  { q: 'Why do we pin exact versions such as playwright==1.64.0?', o: ['So that the code behaves the same on every machine and in CI', 'Because newer versions never work', 'Because pip cannot install the latest version', 'To make the download smaller'], a: 0, why: 'Libraries change. With exact versions, "it works on my machine" also means "it works on yours".' },
  { q: L('Which sign tells you that the Python virtual environment is active?', 'Which file lists the libraries that a Kotlin Gradle project needs?'), o: [L('The prompt starts with (.venv)', 'build.gradle.kts'), L('The prompt shows the folder name', 'settings.gradle.kts'), L('Python prints the word "active"', 'gradlew'), L('A file called pytest.ini exists', 'FirstTest.kt')], a: 0, why: L('Activation changes the prompt. If you do not see it, packages may be installed in the wrong place.', 'The dependencies block of build.gradle.kts names the libraries and their versions, and Gradle downloads them.') },
  { q: 'What does the browser installation step download?', o: ['The browsers that Playwright controls', 'The website under test', 'Your test data', 'The documentation'], a: 0, why: 'Playwright drives its own copies of Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, so that behaviour is the same on every computer.' },
  { type: 'fill', q: 'Which Playwright tool records your clicks and suggests locators? (one word)', ans: ['codegen', 'playwright codegen'], why: 'Codegen writes code while you click. Use it as a hint, then clean the result up.' },
  { q: 'A test fails with "Timeout 30000ms exceeded. waiting for ...". What do you do first?', o: ['Read the Call log, and run the test with a visible browser to see the page', 'Add a sleep of 30 seconds', 'Delete the test', 'Reinstall the operating system'], a: 0, why: 'The call log says what Playwright was waiting for. Watching the page usually shows why it never appeared.' },
  { q: 'Why does the base class (Kotlin) or the plugin (Python) create a new context for every test?', o: ['So that each test starts clean and independent', 'Because creating contexts is free of charge for the page', 'Because the browser would crash otherwise', 'Because a test can only have one page'], a: 0, why: 'A fresh context has no cookies and no saved data, so a test cannot be disturbed by the one before it.' }
],
practice: { id: 'p-m2', title: 'Your first run, and your first failure',
  brief: '<p>On your own computer, follow the steps above until the test passes. Then do three things and write the answers below:</p><ol><li>Run the test <b>headed</b> and describe in one sentence what you saw.</li><li>Break the test on purpose in a <b>different</b> way than I did (for example, a wrong placeholder text). Copy the <b>first line</b> of the error.</li><li>Open the page with <b>codegen</b>, add a second todo, and copy one line of generated code that you find interesting.</li></ol>',
  template: 'What I saw in headed mode:\n\nMy broken test and the first line of its error:\n\nOne line from codegen, and what I think it does:\n',
  checks: ['The first test passes on my computer', 'I ran it headed and saw the browser', 'I caused a failure and read its first line', 'I tried codegen and looked at the code it wrote'],
  model: '<p>Typical answers. <b>Headed:</b> a Chromium window opens, the placeholder gets the text "Buy milk", and a new row appears in the list.</p><p><b>A different failure:</b> with <code>get_by_placeholder("What to do?")</code> (a wrong hint text) the first line is a timeout, <code>TimeoutError: Locator.fill: Timeout 30000ms exceeded</code>, and the call log says <code>waiting for get_by_placeholder("What to do?")</code>. The message is long, but the call log names exactly what was not found.</p><p><b>Codegen:</b> a typical line is <code>page.get_by_placeholder("What needs to be done?").fill("Buy milk")</code>. It finds the input by its hint text and types into it: the same idea as the first test.</p>' }
});

